      "id": 1,
      "text": "Camp Springs was originally called Allentown after the Allen family, but soldiers camping near its springs gave it the name Camp Springs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://campspringsca.org/about-2/history-in-camp-springs/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The crossroads of Branch Avenue and Allentown Road became the heart of the mid‑19th‑century settlement that later grew into Camp Springs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camp_Springs%2C_Maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1860 the community already had a blacksmith, a Methodist church, and a school, showing early self‑sufficiency.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camp_Springs%2C_Maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1940s subdivision of the Middleton farm sparked the first major residential boom north of the crossroads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camp_Springs%2C_Maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The former Camp Springs Elementary School now houses the Camp Springs Senior Activity Center, preserving a historic building for community use.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
